About FIGlet (Frank, Ian & Glenn's Letters) release 2.2.5, 31 May 2012
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIGlet is a program that creates large characters out of ordinary
screen characters
 _ _ _          _   _     _
| (_) | _____  | |_| |__ (_)___
| | | |/ / _ \ | __| '_ \| / __|
| | |   <  __/ | |_| | | | \__ \_
|_|_|_|\_\___|  \__|_| |_|_|___(_)

(This is meant to be viewed in a monospaced font.)  FIGlet can create
characters in many different styles and can kern and "smush" these
characters together in various ways.  FIGlet output is generally
reminiscent of the sort of "signatures" many people like to put at the
end of e-mail and UseNet messages.

If you like FIGlet (hey, even if you *hate* FIGlet), please send an
e-mail message to <info@figlet.org>

The official FIGlet web page: http://www.figlet.org/


About this port
---------------

This is FIGlet 2.2.5 for OpenVMS Alpha. It builds on OpenVMS only. It
uses DCL syntax for its command line, in place of Unix options.

The code comes from the upstream FIGlet repository,
https://github.com/cmatsuoka/figlet, at the commit after the 2.2.5
release. The git history of this repository starts with the upstream
history, so "git diff" against upstream shows each change that the port
made.

Send problems with the OpenVMS port to this repository, not to the
FIGlet authors:  https://github.com/kleinmatic/figlet-vms

Changes from FIGlet 2.2.5 for Unix:
- DCL command syntax in place of Unix options. getopt is not used.
- The font directory comes from the location of the image.
- figlist and showfigfonts are now FIGLET /LIST and FIGLET /SAMPLES.
- /INPUT and /OUTPUT qualifiers.
- The Unix build files, shell scripts, and man pages are removed.
- The code for other operating systems is removed from FIGLET.C,
  ZIPIO.C, and CHKFONT.C.

The first version of the port was written by Google Antigravity
(Gemini). Claude Code (Anthropic) corrected the test procedure and the
DCL. Scott Klein wrote the DCL command syntax.


Files
-----

README          -- This file.
FIGLET.C        -- The FIGlet source code. The command line, the font
                   directory, /LIST and /SAMPLES use OpenVMS routines
                   (CLI$, LIB$).
FIGLET_CLD.CLD  -- The DCL command table. SET COMMAND/OBJECT compiles
                   it and LINK puts it in FIGLET.EXE.
ZIPIO.H,        -- A package for reading ZIP archives
ZIPIO.C,
INFLATE.C,
CRC.C
UTF8.H,         -- A package to convert strings between UTF-8 and UCS-4.
UTF8.C
CHKFONT.C       -- Source code for chkfont: a program that checks FIGlet
                   fonts for formatting errors.  You can ignore this
                   file unless you intend to design or edit fonts.
BUILD_VMS.COM   -- Builds FIGLET.EXE and CHKFONT.EXE with DCL only.
DESCRIP.MMS     -- The same build for MMS or MMK.
TEST_VMS.COM    -- The test procedure. [.TESTS] holds its reference
                   files.
FIGFONT.TXT     -- Text file that explains the format of FIGlet fonts.
                   You can ignore this file unless you intend to design
                   or edit fonts.
CHANGES, FAQ    -- The upstream FIGlet history and questions.
LICENSE         -- The FIGlet licence (BSD).
[.FONTS]        -- Directory containing fonts and control files.
<xxx>.FLF       -- All files ending in ".FLF" are FIGlet font files.
<xxx>.FLC       -- All files ending in ".FLC" are FIGlet control files.


Installing FIGlet
-----------------

You need OpenVMS Alpha 7.0 or later with DEC C, Compaq C, HP C, or
VSI C. MMS or MMK is optional.

Copy this directory tree, with [.FONTS] and [.TESTS], to the OpenVMS
system. Then build it in that directory:

    $ @BUILD_VMS            ! Build FIGLET.EXE and CHKFONT.EXE
    $ @BUILD_VMS NOTLF      ! Build without TOIlet (.TLF) font support
    $ @BUILD_VMS DEBUG      ! Build with /DEBUG/NOOPTIMIZE
    $ @BUILD_VMS CLEAN      ! Delete the .OBJ and .EXE files

or, with MMS or MMK:

    $ MMS
    $ MMS /MACRO=NOTLF=1
    $ MMS /MACRO=DEBUG=1
    $ MMS CLEAN

Then run the tests:

    $ @TEST_VMS

FIGLET.EXE needs no setup procedure and no logical name. It finds its
fonts in the [.FONTS] subdirectory of the directory that contains
FIGLET.EXE. If you move FIGLET.EXE, move [.FONTS] with it.

To run it with no setup at all, use MCR with the full file
specification:

    $ MCR DKA0:[FIGLET]FIGLET "Hello"

To use the short command FIGLET, do one of these steps. OpenVMS needs
one of them for every program that is not part of the system.

    $ FIGLET :== $DKA0:[FIGLET]FIGLET.EXE       ! a foreign command symbol

    $ DEFINE DCL$PATH DKA0:[FIGLET]             ! or: automatic foreign commands

Put the line in LOGIN.COM (one user) or SYS$MANAGER:SYLOGIN.COM (all
users).

Do not use SET COMMAND with FIGLET_CLD.CLD. The command table is
inside FIGLET.EXE.

If space is a problem, the only files you absolutely must have to run
FIGlet are FIGLET.EXE and at least one font in [.FONTS] (preferably
STANDARD.FLF, the default font).


Using FIGlet
------------

(Note: FIGlet needs a good thorough tutorial.  Currently I don't have
the time to write one, but if anyone wants to do so, go right ahead.
I'd be glad to help out a little.  Write us at <ianchai@usa.net> if
you're interested.  -GGC-)

At the DCL prompt, type FIGLET.  Then type, say, Hello, world! and
press Return.  "Hello, world!" in nice, big, designer characters should
appear on your screen:
 _   _      _ _                             _     _ _
| | | | ___| | | ___    __      _____  _ __| | __| | |
| |_| |/ _ \ | |/ _ \   \ \ /\ / / _ \| '__| |/ _` | |
|  _  |  __/ | | (_) |   \ V  V / (_) | |  | | (_| |_|
|_| |_|\___|_|_|\___( )   \_/\_/ \___/|_|  |_|\__,_(_)
                    |/
Then type something else, or type Ctrl/Z to quit FIGlet.

You can also give the message on the command line:

    $ FIGLET "Hello, world!"

Now you can send the output of FIGlet to a file
(FIGLET /OUTPUT=HELLO.TXT "Hello, world!") and e-mail it to your
friends (who will probably say, "Wow!  It must have taken you hours to
put that together!")

To use other fonts, use the /FONT qualifier.  For example, if you had
said FIGLET /FONT=SMSLANT above, you would have seen
   __ __    ____                         __   ____
  / // /__ / / /__      _    _____  ____/ /__/ / /
 / _  / -_) / / _ \_   | |/|/ / _ \/ __/ / _  /_/
/_//_/\__/_/_/\___( )  |__,__/\___/_/ /_/\_,_(_)
                  |/

DCL rules apply to the message:
- Text without quotation marks becomes uppercase:  FIGLET Hello  prints
  HELLO.
- Put the message in quotation marks to keep lowercase letters and
  spaces.
- To put a quotation mark in the message, type two:  "Say ""Hi""".
- You can give up to 8 words without quotation marks. FIGLET puts one
  space between them.
- Qualifier names and keywords are not case-sensitive. You can
  abbreviate them (4 characters are always sufficient). You do not have
  to put them in quotation marks.

If there is no message, FIGLET reads the text from SYS$INPUT (or from
the /INPUT file).

Qualifiers:

    /FONT=name              Font name or file specification. Default:
                            STANDARD.
    /DIRECTORY=dir          Font directory.
    /CONTROL=(file[,...])   Control files (.FLC), applied in the given
                            order.
    /WIDTH=n                Output width in columns. Default: 80, so
                            that people with wide terminals won't annoy
                            the people they e-mail FIGlet output to.
    /TERMINAL               Use the width of the terminal.
    /JUSTIFY=keyword        LEFT, CENTER, or RIGHT. Default: set by the
                            print direction.
    /RIGHT_TO_LEFT          Print right-to-left.
    /NORIGHT_TO_LEFT        Print left-to-right. Default: set by the
                            font.
    /PARAGRAPH              Paragraph mode: a single newline in the
                            input becomes a space. This eliminates some
                            spurious line breaks when you give FIGlet a
                            file with many lines.
    /GERMAN                 Print [ \ ] { | } ~ as German letters.
    /SPACING=keyword        SMUSH, KERN, FULL_WIDTH, or OVERLAP.
                            Default: set by the font.
    /SMUSH_MODE=n           Layout mode number (see FIGFONT.TXT).
    /INPUT=file             Read the text from this file.
    /OUTPUT=file            Write the result to this file.
    /LIST                   List the fonts and control files in the
                            font directory.
    /SAMPLES                Print the message in each font of the font
                            directory. With no message, print each font
                            name.
    /VERSION                Print the version and copyright text.
    /INFO=n                 Print one item: 1 version number, 2 font
                            directory, 3 default font, 4 output width,
                            5 font formats.

You cannot use these qualifiers together: /SPACING with /SMUSH_MODE,
/WIDTH with /TERMINAL, and more than one of /LIST /SAMPLES /VERSION
/INFO.

Examples:

    $ FIGLET "Hello, OpenVMS!"
    $ FIGLET /FONT=SLANT /JUSTIFY=CENTER /WIDTH=132 "Hello"
    $ FIGLET /FONT=BANNER /CONTROL=USKATA "ABCDE"
    $ FIGLET /RIGHT_TO_LEFT /FONT=IVRIT "Hello"
    $ FIGLET /INPUT=MESSAGE.TXT /OUTPUT=BANNER.TXT /PARAGRAPH
    $ FIGLET /LIST
    $ FIGLET /SAMPLES /OUTPUT=SAMPLES.TXT "Alpha"

DEFINE/USER_MODE SYS$INPUT, DEFINE/USER_MODE SYS$OUTPUT, and PIPE also
operate.

The Unix options and the qualifiers that replace them:

    -f font         /FONT=font
    -d dir          /DIRECTORY=dir
    -C file         /CONTROL=file
    -w n            /WIDTH=n
    -t              /TERMINAL
    -l  -c  -r      /JUSTIFY=LEFT  /JUSTIFY=CENTER  /JUSTIFY=RIGHT
    -x              (default; give no /JUSTIFY)
    -R  -L          /RIGHT_TO_LEFT  /NORIGHT_TO_LEFT
    -X              (default; give no /RIGHT_TO_LEFT)
    -p  -n          /PARAGRAPH  (default is no paragraph mode)
    -D  -E          /GERMAN  (default is no German letters)
    -S  -k  -W  -o  /SPACING=SMUSH  =KERN  =FULL_WIDTH  =OVERLAP
    -s              (default; give no /SPACING)
    -m n            /SMUSH_MODE=n
    -v  -I n        /VERSION  /INFO=n
    -N  -A          (not necessary)
    figlist         FIGLET /LIST
    showfigfonts    FIGLET /SAMPLES


Font directory
--------------

FIGLET uses the first of these that applies:

1. The /DIRECTORY qualifier.
2. The logical name FIGLET_FONTS, if it is defined. This is optional.
3. The [.FONTS] subdirectory of the directory that contains FIGLET.EXE.

If /FONT or /CONTROL gives a device or a directory, FIGLET uses that
file specification as it is.

    $ FIGLET /INFO=2        ! shows the font directory in use

Fonts and control files that are not compressed can have any RMS text
record format (VAR, VFC, STMLF, STM, STMCR). FIGLET reads them in text
mode into memory.

ZIP-compressed fonts are read in binary mode. Transfer them in binary
mode (for example FTP BINARY).


CHKFONT
-------

CHKFONT.EXE examines font files for format errors. It has file
parameters only and no qualifiers.

    $ MCR DKA0:[FIGLET]CHKFONT DKA0:[FIGLET.FONTS]SLANT.FLF


Other Fonts & Mailing List
--------------------------

A good number of FIGlet fonts have been developed, most of which are
not included in the standard FIGlet package.  Many of these can be
obtained from http://www.figlet.org/   Some non-Roman fonts are
available at this site.  As of this writing, we have Hebrew, Cyrillic
(Russian) and Greek.

To use one of them on OpenVMS, copy the .FLF file into [.FONTS]. ODS-2
disks do not allow spaces in file names, so rename the file if its name
has spaces.

There are 3 mailing lists available for FIGlet:
	 figlet@figlet.org           General discussion of FIGlet
	 figletfonts@figlet.org      Announcements about fonts
	 figletsoftware@figlet.org   Announcements about software
	 (The last two lists are moderated)

To subscribe or unsubscribe from the FIGlet mailing lists, please visit
the corresponding URL:
	 http://www.figlet.org/mailman/listinfo/figlet
	 http://www.figlet.org/mailman/listinfo/figletfonts
	 http://www.figlet.org/mailman/listinfo/figletsoftware

Also, for those who maintain archives of figlet fonts, please note that
all of the standard fonts have been changed, as of release 2.1, to
include non-ASCII characters.  These fonts are the following:

big.flf (also contains Greek)
banner.flf (also contains Cyrillic and Japanese katakana)
block.flf
bubble.flf
digital.flf
ivrit.flf (right-to-left, also contains Hebrew)
lean.flf
mini.flf
script.flf
shadow.flf
slant.flf
small.flf
smscript.flf
smshadow.flf
smslant.dld
standard.flf
term.flf

The new versions of these fonts can be identified by the words "figlet
release 2.1" somewhere in the first few lines.


Other Stuff
-----------

FIGlet is available for operating systems other than Unix.
Please see ftp://ftp.figlet.org/program/

Although you don't have to design your own fonts to use FIGlet, we'd
certainly like it if lots of people did make up new FIGlet fonts.  If
you feel like giving it a try, see FIGFONT.TXT.  If you do design a
font, please let us know by mailing us at <info@figlet.org>


Authors
-------

FIGlet was written mostly by Glenn Chappell <c486scm@semovm.semo.edu>.  The
author not being an e-mail fanatic, most correspondence (bug reports, rave
reviews, etc.) used to be handled to his secretary (who is definitely
an e-mail fanatic), Ian Chai <ianchai@usa.net> and has since moved on to
another FIGlet enthusiast, Christiaan Keet <info@figlet.org>. Current
maintenance is conducted by Claudio Matsuoka <cmatsuoka@gmail.com>.
